A||N||7||fieroluke||87 L4 nightmare||||8||
Z||000000||fieroluke||10-23-2001||02:27 PM||||Hi folks,<P>I have trouble with a recently rebuilt 87 4-banger.<P>We rebuilt the entire thing, except for the rocker arms and rocker bolts. Well, after 500 miles<BR>of great running, one rocker arm snapped.<BR>No prob. Popped in a set of new rockers, and back on the road. Another few hundred miles<BR>later a (different) rocker arm bolt snapped right off. Okay, bad karma, should have changed it in the first place, put a new set in, car ran great. Except: after a few miles, the engine started ticking. The ticking was not very loud, but noticeable, and decreased with increasing temperature, so I ruled out a <BR>bad crank bearing. Using the stethoscope revealed no suspect. A hundred miles later,<BR>the same (new!) bolt snapped again. The valve<BR>height looks okay, but I lack the special tool, so this is only with a caliper ruler.<P>Put it back together, and thinking that maybe<BR>the valve sits too high due to grinding, I<BR>put a shim under the bolt to raise the rocker<BR>about half a millimeter. Ticking still there,<BR>now the rocker lasted only 20 miles, and the<BR>new rocker snapped, but the pushrod was also<BR>bent. I figure the bent pushrod pushed sideways, thus putting pressure on the rocker<BR>in a direction it wasn't built for.<P>My theory now: for whatever reason, maybe the<BR>pushrod or lifter were slightly damaged, causing a gap between rocker and pushrod. This gap produced the click. As the lifters got filled with oil, the gap got smaller, thus the noise went away. Still, because of the gap the pushrod sort of "slammed" into the rocker suddenly, instead of smoothly pushing it up, thus putting a strain on the rocker & bolt. Now, the bolt is not adjustable, so I am thinking of having my machine shop make a stud for me that I can put on the head to make valve lash adjustable, and then replace lifter, rocker arm and pushrod together, and then adjust<BR>valve lash just like on a V6.<P>Now, does this sound reasonable, or does anybody have any suggestions?<P>Best regards,<P>-fieroluke||||reg||8
Z||000001||CHUCKVH||10-23-2001||02:52 PM||||Sounds like valve spring coil bind to me.  If your cam has excessive lift, or your valve springs are incorrect, installed with too many shims, wrong height etc., the spring coils will contact each other as you approach maximum lift.  Once the spring can no longer compress, something has to give, and it's usually a rocker or pushrod.  If your springs were used, they may have had to shim them to get the correct seat pressure.  You should have clearance between the coils at max lift, otherwise "bang", broken parts.<P>Chuck||||reg||1
Z||000002||88CoupeV6||10-23-2001||03:07 PM||||I would have to agree with CHUCKVH.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroluke||10-23-2001||03:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CHUCKVH:<BR><B>Sounds like valve spring coil bind to me.  If your cam has excessive lift, or your valve springs are incorrect, installed with too many shims, wrong height etc., the spring coils will contact each other as you approach maximum lift. You should have clearance between the coils at max lift, otherwise "bang", broken parts.<P>Chuck</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hi,<P>thanks for the comments.<BR>I thought about that too, but <BR>a) I have a stock cam<BR>b) the valve springs are brand new, and no shims<BR>c) They're from the Fiero Store, so I assume they're correct<BR>d) They were all in the same bag, and the other ones work fine<BR>e) Even this one worked fine, until the first  <BR>bolt snapped<P>But I will try to turn the crank manually to check if the coil binds with max. lift...<BR>That is, as soon as the new parts arrive...<P>Thanks,<P>-Oliver||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-23-2001||07:31 PM||||Exactly what machine work was done to the block and head?<P>planing the block and head combined with increased valve seat recess could make a significant change to valve geometry on this head.<P>Did you make and cam/lifter changes? Is it the same bolt breaking or differant ones?<P>It does sound like something is binding. hard to say exactly what w/o seeing it.<P>Are you sure they didn't send you heavier springs? That could also cause a problem like this. If the springs or valves aren't binding then I'd check what the spring rate is.<P>A loose rocker or a bent pushrod usually will not break things. Only if it's loose enough to jump the lifter or rocker completely would it likely cause damage.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroluke||10-24-2001||02:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Exactly what machine work was done to the block and head?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The block was bored and honed, the head was<BR>okay. No planing. Only one valve needed grinding more than the others (don't know if this is the one though)<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>Did you make and cam/lifter changes? Is it the same bolt breaking or differant ones?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, it's a stock cam/lifters (but new ones),<BR>and it's the same bolt breaking.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>It does sound like something is binding. hard to say exactly what w/o seeing it.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It can't be seen.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>Are you sure they didn't send you heavier springs? That could also cause a problem like this. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's hard to tell, they came in an<BR>unmarked bag.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>If the springs or valves aren't binding then I'd check what the spring rate is.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't have the tools for that. And why only<BR>this one, and not all of them? The engine ran<BR>fine for close to 1000 miles...<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>A loose rocker or a bent pushrod usually will not break things. Only if it's loose enough to jump the lifter or rocker completely would it likely cause damage.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is my suspicion. Is 1-2 mm of jumping enough to cause this kind of damage?<BR>And will I avoid this with an adjustable rocker?<P>Best regards,<P>-Oliver<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||CHUCKVH||10-24-2001||03:27 PM||||Have you checked the valve stem for signs of seizing?  I have seen valve stems that after running for a while would seize so tight that they caused the type of damage you describe.  Usually they don't loosen up after cooling down, but its worth a shot to check it out.  You'll have to remove the valve spring to do it.  You can do it with the head installed by filling the cylinder with nylon rope.<P>Chuck||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-24-2001||07:05 PM||||The same bolt/rocker breaking pretty much says that valve has a problem.<P>The valve stem can bind. Posible if they botched the valve job. <P>That lifter could be bad. Very posible. The lifter could actually be made bad or become contaminated by stuff that wasn't washed out of the motor durring rebuild. Either way it would have to be replaced.<P>If the lifters aren't pushing oil up the pushrods then you could be running the rockers dry and gall/bind the main pivot. you'd likely be able to see that and it would probably take longer to break that 20-50 miles. You'd probably be able to see significant damage on the pivot.<P>All the valves should have been reground exactly the same. If one valve is setup different that could be a problem but you should be able to see that valve sitting taller if that's the case.||||reg||1
